# Flutter Flavors and Environment Config

This rule is the constraint. The skill `flutter-setup-flavors` is the workflow for a single environment, several flavors, or one more flavor. Both follow the Flutter Architecture rule.

Use the flavor names the project already has. Do not create, rename, or remove a flavor unless the request asks for it. Do not assume a flavor exists on both Android and iOS; check the native setup first.

## Flavor selection

- Each entry point selects its flavor explicitly and passes it to the shared bootstrap in `app/`, which passes it to the DI setup as the `injectable` environment. Entry points contain no other startup code.
- Never fall back to another flavor when a value or a flavor is missing. Never treat a missing or unknown flavor as production.
- A production build never reads development or pre-production values.
- A flavor has the same name in its entry point, its `injectable` environment, its `.env` file, the Android `productFlavors`, and the iOS scheme and build configurations.

## Configuration in core/config

- The app depends on an `AppConfig` class in `core/config/`: base URLs, public identifiers, environment feature flags, and other non-secret settings.
- Each flavor has one `AppConfig` implementation registered with `@LazySingleton(as: AppConfig, env: ['<flavor>'])`. A project with a single environment has one implementation without `env`.
- Only those implementations import the generated `envied` classes. Services, datasources, repositories, use cases, Cubits, and widgets inject `AppConfig` or a service that uses it. They never read an `Env` class, `String.fromEnvironment`, or the flavor name.
- Do not branch on the flavor (`if (flavor == 'prod')`) outside `core/config/`. Put the difference in `AppConfig`.
- A value that is the same in every flavor lives once in `AppConfig`, not in every `.env` file.

## envied

- One `.env.<flavor>` file and one `@Envied(path: '.env.<flavor>')` class per flavor in `core/config/env/`. A single environment uses `.env` and one class.
- Required keys have no default, so a missing key fails generation. Use `optional: true` only for a value that is truly optional.
- Use `obfuscate: true` for public keys that should not appear as plain text. Obfuscation does not make a value secret.
- Git ignores every `.env` file except the `.example` files, and the generated `*.g.dart` files in `core/config/env/`, because they contain the values. Each `.env` file has a committed `.example` twin with the same keys and empty values.
- After changing a `.env` file, run `dart run build_runner clean` before the project's build command; otherwise `build_runner` can keep stale values. Never edit generated env files.
- CI writes each `.env` file from its secret store before running `build_runner`.

## Secrets

- Anything compiled into the app can be extracted, with or without obfuscation. Never put server credentials, private API keys, database passwords, or signing secrets in a `.env` file the app reads. They belong on a backend.
- Never commit real values. Never log tokens, keys, or config values.
- Runtime user credentials go through the secure storage service in `core/storage/`, not through environment config.

## Done

The change is done when each entry point selects its flavor explicitly, config comes from `AppConfig`, no code outside `core/config/` reads an `Env` class or branches on the flavor, `.env` files and generated env files are ignored with an `.example` committed, and no secret is compiled into the app. State which flavors were built or run, and which could not be.
